n't practicing the  thing you want to remember so take  breaks and get lots of sleep but how  exactly does this relate to gamers using  preposterous peripherals well allow me  one more detour into the realm of music  since long before there were heads to  click in your fps of choice musicians  have been learning and mastering some  incredibly complex and intricate hand  and finger movements and just like  gamers we see musicians gravitate  towards different shapes and sizes of  their chosen instrument even though  they're putting those same notes and  chords into memory each person's fingers  hands wrists and so on are all different  and they will tend to play those notes  and chords in different more comfortable  ways and that right there is the key  that could actually help you now am i  saying you should be slamming out your  valor and ranked games on a set of rock  band drums i mean [ __ ] if you can do  that you should definitely do that that  would be cool as hell  right that's not the point what  experimenting with weird peripherals can  do is give you some insights into what  sorts of movements shapes and positions  are most comfortable so you can find  peripherals that work best and help you  reach the top of your game and that was  the guy who actually knows what he's  talking about whether it's for the  purposes of accessibility or just pure  entertainment the fighting game  community in particular has used some of  the weirdest peripherals at events back  in 2014  a street fighter 4 player named luffy  shocked the world when he won evo with a  playstation 1 controller and luffy uses  some meter for an ex fireball so no more  soul spiral in the supers but bonchan  also had a meter  oh no oh no  he has taken evolution 2014. now some of  you may be thinking that a ps1 gamepad  actually seems pretty tame but let me  assure you for a game like street  fighter  it is very out of the ordinary since the  playstation 1 is basically  ancient at this point luffy had to use a  lag-free ps1 converter to play the game  properly while his opponents just had to  plug in their fight sticks or pads some  street fighter competitors like santorum  and have actually memed on him for his  controller selection  since then we've seen even crazier  peripherals pop up in the fgc from  guitar hero controllers to gamecube  keyboards and whatever the hell this is  fighting game enthusiasts have perhaps  taken things a little too far but at the  2018 canada cup a player named initial t  did the unthinkable he managed to take  down the 2017 guilty gear champion  dogura with a steering wheel yes you  heard me this dude plays guilty gear  with a  steering wheel hey goes for a good time  a baby conversion the ghetto combo does  get a combo hard knocked out just do  super the club did this get him it got  him  [Applause]  he lost his burst there every time oh my  goodness now thanks to an interview he  did with gerald of core a gaming we were  given a little insight as to why initial  tv does this so it all kind of started  on our local discord  i bought this to actually play racing  games on  but when i showed it to people on  discord they were just like but can you  play fighting games on it and i was like  why not i'll give it a try so i booted  up guilty gear with this tried some  hanging combos and i was just like okay  it works well enough  so then everybody started egging me on  oh you better bring this to cat in the  cup and better play your pools with this  so i was like okay i guess i'm doing it  i know what all of you are wondering  does initial t control his character by  physically turning the steering wheel  sadly  the answer is no although he is able to  control the side to side movement of his  character by turning the wheel he opts  to use the d-pad for movement and the  paddle shifter and other buttons to  fight but while the fighting game  community continues to try out these  large and clunky peripherals competitors  from other e