ng that for a very long time  it's just like what's comfortable to me  and  it's something i've gotten used to so  i'm not really comfortable playing on a  different setup i can move the left side  separately from the right side so i can  like put it wherever i want on the desk  and i have like empty space in the  middle i just like that better than  having the keyboard take up all the  space  but these  odd play styles and peripherals aren't  exclusive to fighting games and hero  shooters no no matter what game you play  comfort is key to performing at your  best and within the speed running  community this is especially true when  every second and sometimes even  milliseconds can make all the difference  players are always looking for ways to  improve their efficiency within the  server and among the endless sea of  speed running savants is a man who goes  by the moniker angelus deminus he runs  the most difficult solo content in final  fantasy xiv despite the fact that said  content was never actually designed to  be soloed hell nobody even managed to  solo some of this content for nearly two  years after the deep dungeons release a  single run in the original deep dungeon  can take players anywhere from 12 to 16  hours to complete and about half that  time for the follow up version heaven on  high now these time limited runs are an  exercise in risk management and they're  extremely unforgiving if at any point  you happen to die then you must start  all the way back from the beginning  barring specific rare items that need to  be conserved to succeed there are no  second chances oh and not to mention  that there are randomized layouts and  items so rng can also play a huge factor  and angelus demonas sits second on the  ether solo leaderboard but what is  particularly impressive is that he has  secured this spot  without the use of a mouse first off uh  we have my general movement and my  general movement is the basic super  standard w a s d so c is just the normal  movement w is forward s is back uh a d  is left and right and this is where it  gets top um interesting is with my  camera movement my camera movement is  set to uh the minus sign where the cam  camera moves up the open brackets where  the camera moves down uh p spins the  camera to the left and then the closed  brackets spin the camera to the right  now the vast majority of pc mmo players  are accustomed to the standard mouse and  keyboard so we decided to sit down with  angelus and ask him why he opted to  forgo the former at the time 11 came out  it was when i was in college and i was a  lot of times i was in college and stuff  so i had a laptop on me  um and it was just convenient that way  but 11  was not the greatest with the mouse  support it just was more like a  controller game-ish but also when you  kind of use the mouse it was just kind  of dull so i kind of just figured out a  way to do with my keyboard and that's  kind of how it first started that  adaptation became part of his play style  and when 14 released it featured a lot  of accessibility options to support both  gamepad and pc peripherals so he was  able to replicate his experience in the  newer mmo even if it isn't everyone's  cup of tea having both hands and your  keyboard can have some benefits like  more precise targeting one of the worst  parts of ff14's interface and let's not  forget this is a challenge where every  second counts  my spins down  okay  i'm gonna time out on 100  i'm gonna timeout on 100 i don't have  enough time to reach the to exit and go  oh my god  i don't have anything  this is going to be the tragic ending  i'm not making it  what oh my god  what angelus demonis might not have  expected is that his strange method of  playing the game has actually made final  fantasy much more accessible to other  players and even if they don't adopt his  playstyle as a whole they can learn  lessons about how to tweak their own but  no matter what type of games you play  you can a